---
import SwitchCase from "@/components/ui/display/SwitchCase.astro";
import type { TransferableSkillMeta } from "@/utils/transferableSkills";

export const meta: TransferableSkillMeta = {
	title: "Team Player",
	summary: "Adaptability, trust and shared goals - leading developers while collaborating with sales and design under deadline pressure.",
	icon: "ph:users-three-duotone",
	featured: true,
	order: 40,
	keywords: ["collaboration", "teamwork", "adaptability", "cross-functional"],
};

interface Props {
	only?: string;
}
const { only } = Astro.props;
---

<SwitchCase value={only}>
	<Fragment slot="card">
		<p>
			While competition can inspire excellence, collaboration is far more powerful, enabling teams to achieve more together than they could individually. My experience managing and being part of
			teams has shown me the value of adaptability, trust, and shared goals in navigating challenges and ambiguity. As a lead developer at a digital agency startup, I balanced multiple roles,
			guiding a team of developers and working closely with sales and design teams to ensure smooth project completion. I simplified complex ideas for stakeholders, designed reusable tools for
			efficiency, and aligned projects with time and budget constraints to deliver quality results.
		</p>
	</Fragment>
	<Fragment slot="default">
		<p class="text-lg">Collaboration is more powerful than Competition</p>

		<p>
			Although I enjoy a healthy amount of competition, it's only useful if it pushes the team members to bring out their best and collaborate further. I have both managed and been a part of
			teams, and the one thing I've learnt is that <strong>the whole is greater than the sum of its parts</strong>.... together we can do more than we can apart.
		</p>

		<p>Being adaptable and working through ambiguity is essential, trusting your team member to do their part and helping them to reach your shared goal is a must.</p>

		<div class="mt-6 rounded border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5">
			<h3 class="mb-3 text-lg">Adapting to what the team needs</h3>
			<p>
				For 12 months, (2016 - 2017) I sub-contracted to a digital agency startup (8 team members) as their lead developer. I was in charge of a small team of developers, working closely with
				the sales and design team to manage deliveries and as a result, I had to wear many 'hats' to ensure smooth completion.
			</p>
			<p>
				I was required to show the design team what was possible within the timeframe and designated budget, create tools and templates the team could use to complete the project with quality
				code, and deliver clear results to the directors and customers in a concise way, without overcomplicating it with technical terminology.
			</p>
		</div>
	</Fragment>
</SwitchCase>
